    ],
    "bottomLine": "Convenient and fairly priced, but bulk Gold Standard often beats it per gram.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "25 g per scoop (label)",
      "Approx. cost per 25 g": "About $1.10-1.40",
      "Third-party testing": "None found",
      "Best for": "Grab-off-the-shelf buyers"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Cheap Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-cheap-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the cheapest protein powder per gram?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Unflavored soy protein isolate is usually the cheapest. At typical prices, BulkSupplements' soy isolate works out to roughly $0.75 to $0.90 per 25 g of protein. Among wheys, Myprotein Impact Whey on a deep sale and 5 lb tubs of mainstream whey come closest. Prices move constantly, so check the cost per 25 g on the day you buy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I calculate cost per 25 g of protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Multiply the protein per serving by the number of servings to get the total protein in the container. Divide the price by that total, then multiply by 25. For example, a tub with 74 servings of 24 g holds 1,776 g of protein; at $85, that is about $1.20 per 25 g of protein."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is cheap protein powder safe?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Mainstream budget powders from established brands are generally fine. The risks are amino spiking, counterfeit or expired product from unknown sellers, and heavy metals, which affect plant powders more on average. Choose brands that publish testing or carry certification, buy from reputable sellers, and see a doctor first if you have kidney disease or are pregnant."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is amino spiking?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Labs usually estimate protein by measuring nitrogen. Amino spiking means adding cheap nitrogen-containing ingredients such as glycine or taurine, which push up the measured protein without adding much muscle-building value. Watch for amino acid blends or proprietary matrices on the label, and for protein per gram that is higher than the protein source could contain."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Subscribe and Save worth it for protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, yes. Amazon's Subscribe and Save typically takes about 5 percent off, up to 15 percent with five or more subscriptions in one delivery, and some products offer more. The price charged is the price when the order ships, so check the reminder email and skip a month if the price has jumped."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it cheaper to buy protein powder in bulk?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Almost always per gram. A 5 lb tub usually costs noticeably less per 25 g of protein than a 2 lb tub of the same product, because packaging and shipping are spread over more powder. Only buy big once you know you like the flavor, and store it sealed somewhere cool and dry."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "When is protein powder cheapest?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Big sale events bring the deepest discounts: Prime Day in July, Amazon's October deal event, Black Friday and Cyber Monday, and January. Brand sites such as Myprotein run frequent sales too. Price-history tools such as camelcamelcamel and Keepa show whether a sale price is genuinely low or just a reset list price."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder",
    tags: [
      "best cheap protein powder",
      "cheap protein powder",
      "bulk protein powder",
      "discounted protein powder",
      "protein powder sale",
      "budget protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Cost-per-Gram Ranked
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Cheap <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Protein Powder</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          The cheapest tub is rarely the cheapest protein. We ranked budget powders on what actually matters, cost per 25 g of protein, and show you the maths so you can check any deal yourself.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#bulksupplements-soy-protein-isolate"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#bulk"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Cost Method
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">{author.name}</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">{author.role}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#cost-method" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Cost per 25 g Method</a>
          <a href="#what-cheap-means" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Counts as Cheap</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#bulk" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Buying in Bulk</a>
          <a href="#sales" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Subscribe and Save, Sales</a>
          <a href="#too-cheap" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Too Cheap: Red Flags</a>
          <a href="#amazon-sellers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Buying Cheap on Amazon</a>
          <a href="#whey-vs-plant" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Cheap Whey vs Cheap Plant</a>
          <a href="#trade-offs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. What You Give Up</a>
          <a href="#food" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. When Food Is Cheaper</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Price per tub tells you almost nothing. A cheap 1.75 lb canister can cost more per gram of protein than a 5 lb tub that looks expensive, and a powder priced far below everyone else may not contain the protein it claims. The only number that settles it is cost per 25 g of protein, and once you know how to work it out, the real bargains are easy to spot.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="cost-method" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Rank: Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every powder on this page is ranked on one number: what you pay for 25 g of protein. We use 25 g because it is a sensible single serving for most adults and a common label dose, so the figure is easy to picture. Price per tub ignores size, and price per serving ignores the fact that one brand's serving is 10 g of protein and another's is 30 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            The method takes thirty seconds. Find the protein per serving and the number of servings on the label, and multiply them to get the total grams of protein in the container. Divide the price by that total, then multiply by 25. That is your cost per 25 g of protein, and it lets you compare a 1 kg pouch of soy isolate with a 5 lb tub of whey on equal terms.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Step</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Example: 5 lb whey tub</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Result</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1. Protein per serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g, per the label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2. Servings per container</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">74, per the label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">74</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3. Total protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g times 74</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,776 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4. Price (example only)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Say the tub costs $85</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$85</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5. Cost per 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$85 divided by 1,776, times 25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.20</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The $85 in that table is an example to show the arithmetic, not a quote; real prices change weekly, sometimes daily. That is why we give approximate ranges throughout this guide instead of exact prices. Run the same five steps on the listing in front of you and you will know within a minute whether a deal is as good as it looks.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one trap. Some brands list a serving as two scoops, and some list protein per scoop when the label serving is larger. Always use the protein and serving count from the same nutrition panel, not the big number on the front of the tub. And if the protein per serving looks implausibly high for the scoop weight, read the section on amino spiking below before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-cheap-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Counts as Cheap Protein Powder Right Now
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey has become noticeably more expensive over the past couple of years as demand for high-protein foods has surged, and the bargain-bin prices people remember from a decade ago are gone. At typical prices at the time of writing, we think of roughly $1 per 25 g of protein or less as genuinely cheap, $1 to $1.50 as good value, and anything above $2 as premium, whatever the marketing says.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unflavored plant isolates, soy above all, tend to sit at the bottom of the range. Large tubs of mainstream whey concentrate and whey blends usually land between $1 and $1.50 per 25 g, dropping below $1 on a good sale. Whey isolates, clear whey, grass-fed and certified brands generally cost more, and ready-to-drink shakes cost the most per gram of all.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Price Tiers per 25 g of Protein</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Under $1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Genuinely cheap</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1-1.50</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Good value</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.50-2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mid-range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Over $2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Premium</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those tiers are a guide, not a rule, and they will drift as prices move. What does not change is the relationship between them. Bigger containers almost always cost less per gram than smaller ones, unflavored usually costs less than flavored, and whey concentrate costs less than isolate. If a powder breaks all three of those patterns and still undercuts everyone, ask why.
          </p>

          <p>
